# Build context is backend/, so this is the only .dockerignore Docker reads —
# one exists per context root, and a .dockerignore in a subfolder is ignored.
#
# The point is the build cache. Anything sent to the daemon is also sent to
# every build, and a changed file invalidates the layer it sits in. bin/ and
# obj/ are the worst offenders: megabytes of build output that change on every
# single compile, which would bust the restore layer on every build.

# Build output
bin/
obj/
[Dd]ebug/
[Rr]elease/
x64/
x86/
*.user
*.suo
*.userprefs

# Test and coverage output
TestResults/
*.trx
*.coverage
coverage*.json
coverage*.xml
coverage*.info

# Nested build output, for any project that nests its own
**/bin/
**/obj/

# Version control and editor noise
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~

# Local secrets. The connection string and the JWT key live in user-secrets on
# the developer's machine, never in the repo, and must never reach a layer: a
# secret in an image is readable by anyone who can pull it, and image layers are
# not scrubbed by deleting a later file.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pfx
*.pem
*.key
my.ini
secrets.json

# Not needed to build or run the API
*.md
docs/
README.md
.gitignore
.dockerignore
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
!Dockerfile
